-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Viewing and Reading
You may read, inspect, and view the Work publicly on its official repository
strictly for your personal informational purposes.

In plain English:
You are allowed to read and inspect these files on GitHub.

6. Academic and Journalistic Citation
You may quote, cite, and reproduce brief excerpts from the Research Materials
for academic papers, journalistic reports, or technical reviews, provided that:
(a) You do not reproduce more than 250 continuous words or more than one (1)
    diagram or table per review [OWNER TO CONFIRM];
(b) You provide prominent attribution to "ARCUS by ToxArch" with a direct link
    to https://github.com/ToxArch/arcus; and
(c) The citation is not used to create a competing product, service, or
    commercial publication.

In plain English:
You can quote small snippets or one diagram in a research paper or article, as
long as you give clear credit and a link to this repository.

Risk / Enforceability Note:
Section 52(1)(a) of the Indian Copyright Act, 1957 provides a statutory "fair
dealing" exception for criticism, review, and reporting. You cannot restrict
citations narrower than what Indian fair dealing law already permits.

7. Personal and Non-Commercial Academic Study
You may download and review the Research Materials on a single local computer
solely for personal educational study or unpaid university coursework, provided
no live network deployment, production subscriber traffic, or commercial entity
benefits from such study.

In plain English:
Students and researchers can study these papers on their personal computer for
school or university classes for free, as long as it is not for a business.

8. Repository Forking
[OPTIONAL - OWNER TO CONFIRM]
You may fork this repository within GitHub solely for the purpose of personal
reading, local study, or preparing a Contribution to submit back to the
Licensor. Any public redistribution of a fork outside GitHub, or use of a fork
for unauthorized Commercial Use or Production Use, is strictly prohibited.

In plain English:
You can click the "Fork" button on GitHub to read or propose changes to the
project, but you cannot use that fork to launch a competing product.

Risk / Enforceability Note:
GitHub's Terms of Service (Section D.4) require public repository owners to
grant all GitHub users the right to fork the repository within GitHub. This
clause aligns with GitHub's platform rules while barring external deployment.
[VERIFY interaction with GitHub Terms of Service].

