26. Moral Rights
To the maximum extent permitted by applicable law, You agree not to assert
against the Licensor, its transferees, or licensees any moral rights (such as
the right of integrity or objection to modification) that would restrict the
Licensor from adapting, updating, or relicensing Your Contribution.

In plain English:
You agree not to sue the owner later complaining about how they edited, updated,
or combined your contribution.

Risk / Enforceability Note:
Under Section 57 of the Indian Copyright Act, 1957, authors possess statutory
"special rights" (moral rights) to claim authorship and restrain distortion or
mutilation. Indian courts have historically held that moral rights cannot be
wholly waived in advance by contract (Amar Nath Sehgal v. Union of India).
However, an agreement not to assert these rights in relation to routine code
refactoring is standard practice [VERIFY Section 57 Indian Copyright Act].

