36. Purpose Limitation
The Work's attribution, subscriber identification, and traffic analysis
techniques are intended strictly for defensive network security, botnet
suppression, and lawful response to authorized cybercrime notices. You shall
not employ the Work for unlawful surveillance, stalkerware, political
tracking, unauthorized eavesdropping, or privacy invasion.

In plain English:
This system is meant for stopping botnets and answering police inquiries, not
spying on regular people.

Risk / Enforceability Note:
"Ethical use" and "purpose limitation" clauses are notoriously difficult to
enforce legally in court because proving an operator's subjective intent is
challenging. However, this clause allows the owner to terminate permission if
egregious surveillance is uncovered.

37. Operator Custody and Privacy Responsibility
Operators alone are the data fiduciaries for their subscribers' personally
identifiable information (PII). Operators bear exclusive legal responsibility
for securing subscriber databases, obtaining necessary consents under DPDP
2023, and complying with statutory retention standards. The Licensor has zero
access to, custody over, or responsibility for any subscriber data processed
by third-party deployments of the Work.

In plain English:
The ISP alone is legally responsible for protecting customer personal data.
The creator of ARCUS never touches or sees customer records.

